To get the CxD system running in the cloud using Docker, you’ll follow a three-step process: preparing the files, setting up the server, and launching the containers.

Since your project involves a C core and a React frontend, this method is the most "streamlined" because it handles the compilation and networking for you.

Step 1: Prepare the Project Files
On your local computer (where you write your code), ensure your folder structure looks like this:

cxd-project/ (Main folder)

Dockerfile (The blueprint for the C core)

docker-compose.yml (The orchestrator)

libcxd/ (Your C source code folder)

cxd-pwa/ (Your React project folder)

The Files

Paste the code provided in our previous conversation into these two files.

Dockerfile: This compiles your C library into a "container image."

docker-compose.yml: This tells Docker to run the C engine and the React PWA side-by-side, and specifically creates the RAM disk for the "Wipe" protocol.

Step 2: Provision a Server (The Cloud)
Sign up for a provider (DigitalOcean, AWS, or Azure).

Create an "Instance" or "Droplet":

OS: Choose Ubuntu 24.04.

Size: At least 2GB of RAM (since you are dedicating 2GB to the CxD RAM disk).

Install Docker: Once you log into your server via terminal (SSH), run this "one-liner" to install everything:

Bash
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh && sudo sh get-docker.sh
Step 3: Launch the CxD System
Now, you need to get your code onto that cloud server and start it.

1. Upload your code

The easiest way is to use Git. On your server:

Bash
git clone https://github.com/YourUsername/cxd-project.git
cd cxd-project
2. Build and Run

This is the "magic" command. It reads the YAML file, builds the C library, sets up the RAM disk, and starts the web server:

Bash
sudo docker-compose up --build -d
--build: Forces it to re-compile your C code.

-d: "Detached" mode (runs in the background so it doesn't stop when you close your terminal).

How to Manage Your Node
Once it is running, you can use these commands to check on the system:

Check Status: sudo docker-compose ps (Shows if the engine and frontend are "Up").

View Logs: sudo docker-compose logs -f cxd-node (Watch the libcxd engine process votes in real-time).

Stop Everything: sudo docker-compose down (This will automatically wipe the RAM disk, destroying any transient data as per your protocol).
